/* Pass 22B. Canon-exact Fallout 4 + Harry Potter revisions. */
(function(){'use strict';const lib=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!lib||!Array.isArray(lib.series))return;const changes={"what-the-armor-leaves-behind":{"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-06":{"number":6,"title":"Steel and Scrap","content":"The Brotherhood did not arrive the next day.\n\nOr the day after that.\n\nA working transmitter did not mean a vertibird would materialize the moment Danse wanted one.\n\nHaylen had repaired the transmitter twice before the reply finally came through. The first message had been nothing but static and a clipped confirmation code. The second included a revised pickup window, subject to weather and aircraft availability.\n\nDanse wanted to send another request. I could see him composing the words in his head.\n\n“Would it help?” Haylen asked.\n\n“It would remind command that our position remains exposed.”\n\n“They know. I sent the vulnerability report. Another message won't make a rotor turn faster.”\n\nRhys was sorting ammunition by the doorway. “Maybe the Brotherhood should try sending two vertibirds and see if one gets here before dinner.”\n\n“Nobody is flying across a radstorm for your appetite,” Haylen said without looking up.\n\nHe opened his mouth, considered the prospects of winning, and went back to counting.\n\nDanse stared at the message for a long moment. Then he asked Haylen which components still needed shielding before the rain. She told him, and they made a list together.\n\nIt was the first time I saw his command voice become a question instead of an instruction. He was still precise; he had not turned into a man who accepted delays cheerfully. But Haylen knew that radio better than anyone else at the station, and when she said the receiver needed ten minutes without interruption, he gave her fifteen.\n\n“You're getting good at listening,” I told him.\n\nHe glanced at me. “I am attempting to avoid wasting specialist time.”\n\n“Of course you are.”\n\nI didn't say it was romantic. It wasn't, not yet. It was simply a way to stay alive while we waited. The station remained what it had been: three exhausted soldiers, one persistent outsider, and a building held together by scavenged wire, habit, and stubbornness.\n\nI stayed.\n\nAt first I told myself it was because Cambridge had walls.\n\nThen because Haylen needed help repairing the signal amplifier.\n\nThen because the workbench was better than mine.\n\nBy the time I ran out of practical excuses, Danse had started setting aside coffee for me.\n\nIt was terrible coffee.\n\nThat did not seem to matter.\n\n“You boil it too long,” I told him one morning.\n\nHe stood at the small hot plate in the back room, watching a dented pot with tactical suspicion.\n\n“It’s coffee.”\n\n“That is not a defense.”\n\n“It contains caffeine.”\n\n“So does a lot of medicine. I don’t want that in a mug either.”\n\nHe poured two cups.\n\n“You complain every time.”\n\n“And yet you keep giving it to me.”\n\n“You keep drinking it.”\n\nWe looked at each other.\n\nHe had me there.\n\nI took the cup.\n\n“Thank you.”\n\n“You’re welcome.”\n\nThe exchange should not have felt domestic.\n\nThe station itself prevented that. Rhys was cleaning a rifle at the front desk while muttering about perimeter discipline. Haylen had fallen asleep over a terminal with one cheek pressed against her sleeve. A generator coughed outside. Somewhere in the walls, a radroach scratched with criminal confidence.\n\nStill, standing across from Danse in the yellow morning light, drinking terrible coffee he had made without being asked, I felt something settle.\n\nHe noticed me looking toward Haylen.\n\n“She was up most of the night.”\n\n“So were you.”\n\n“I slept.”\n\n“When?”\n\n“Enough.”\n\n“That is not a time.”\n\nHis eyes narrowed.\n\n“You keep unusually detailed track of my sleep schedule.”\n\n“You make it easy by barely having one.”\n\nHe took a drink.\n\nHis face did not react to the coffee at all.\n\nThat might have been the most disturbing thing about him.\n\nAfter breakfast, he put me through firearms drills.\n\nI had not agreed to firearms drills.\n\nApparently remaining within fifteen feet of Paladin Danse for multiple consecutive days constituted implied consent to training.\n\n“Again,” he said.\n\nI lowered Righteous Authority.\n\n“I hit all six targets.”\n\n“Your grouping is inconsistent.”\n\n“They are cans.”\n\n“Today they’re cans. Tomorrow they could be synths.”\n\n“Do synths often stand in neat rows on fence posts?”\n\n“Again.”\n\nI muttered something rude.\n\n“What was that?”\n\n“I said ‘outstanding.’”\n\n“Your sarcasm needs work.”\n\nI fired again.\n\nThe last can jumped from the fence.\n\nDanse inspected the grouping.\n\n“Better.”\n\nI stared at him until he added, “Much better.”\n\n“See? Growth.”\n\n“In you, perhaps.”\n\n“Hopeless.”\n\nHis eyes warmed.\n\nThen a noise came from the street.\n\nThe change in him was instant.\n\nDanse moved between me and the doorway before either of us knew what the sound was. His hand went to his sidearm. His body blocked mine on pure reflex.\n\nA brahmin wandered past the entrance wearing half a shopping cart as luggage.\n\nSilence.\n\nI looked at Danse’s back.\n\nHe slowly lowered his weapon.\n\n“That,” I said, “was terrifying.”\n\n“The animal could have been accompanied.”\n\n“By what? An organized raider dairy operation?”\n\nHe stepped aside.\n\n“You mock caution until the day caution saves your life.”\n\n“I’m not mocking caution.”\n\n“You’re laughing.”\n\n“I’m mocking you.”\n\n“That’s becoming a pattern.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHe seemed about to say something else when his attention shifted past me.\n\nRhys stood in the doorway.\n\n“Paladin. Patrol?”\n\nDanse’s expression hardened back into command.\n\n“Five minutes.”\n\nRhys nodded and disappeared.\n\nThe warmth vanished with him.\n\nNot because Danse was pretending around me.\n\nBecause command was another suit of armor, and he wore it whenever someone under him was watching.\n\nI understood that more each day.\n\nThe patrol took us through Cambridge toward an old medical building. Haylen had marked it as a possible source of antiseptics and circuitry. Rhys came with us, limping less than before and resenting me more efficiently.\n\nInside, we found raiders.\n\nThe fight was ugly and close.\n\nAt one point Rhys pushed through a doorway before Danse cleared the second angle. A shotgun blast caught him across the shoulder plate and spun him sideways.\n\nDanse’s response was immediate.\n\nHe put two laser shots through the shooter, dragged Rhys behind cover, and turned on him.\n\n“What the hell were you thinking?”\n\n“I had the room.”\n\n“You had half the room.”\n\n“I saw—”\n\n“You saw what was in front of you. That’s not the same thing.”\n\nRhys’s face tightened.\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n\nDanse checked the wound himself.\n\nHis hands were careful despite the anger.\n\nAfterward, on the walk back, he barely spoke.\n\nRhys went ahead.\n\nI stayed beside Danse.\n\n“You saved him.”\n\n“He shouldn’t have needed saving.”\n\n“People make mistakes.”\n\n“Not all mistakes are survivable.”\n\nThe words came too quickly.\n\nI looked at him.\n\nHe was staring at Rhys’s back.\n\n“Is this about the four names?”\n\nHis jaw tightened.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nThe admission surprised both of us.\n\nWe walked several steps in silence.\n\n“I read them,” I said. “Brach. Worwick. Dawes. Keane.”\n\nDanse stopped.\n\nThe street around us was empty except for weeds moving in the wind.\n\nI expected him to be angry.\n\nInstead he looked tired.\n\n“Keane died here,” he said. “Right outside the station. The others…”\n\nHe trailed off.\n\n“You don’t have to tell me.”\n\n“I know.”\n\nThat mattered.\n\nHe looked toward the station in the distance.\n\n“Maybe I should.”\n\nNot now, his face said.\n\nBut someday.\n\nI nodded.\n\nWe started walking again.\n\nAs we reached the barricade, Danse held the door for me.\n\nAn absurdly ordinary gesture.\n\nI passed him and said, “Thanks.”\n\n“Don’t make anything of it.”\n\n“I wasn’t.”\n\n“You were smiling.”\n\n“I smile sometimes.”\n\n“I’ve noticed.”\n\nHe said it without thinking.\n\nThen his expression changed.\n\nSo did mine.\n\nRhys called from inside before either of us could decide what to do with the sentence.\n\nDanse cleared his throat.\n\n“After you.”\n\nI walked into the station.\n\nBehind me, the door shut with a heavy steel click.\n\nThe sound felt suspiciously like a line being crossed.\n\nLiving at the station—because apparently that was what I was doing now—taught me the shape of Danse’s days.\n\nHe woke before everyone else.\n\nI discovered this because I woke early once to the soft clink of free weights in what had once been an evidence room. Danse was inside in a sleeveless undershirt, counting repetitions under his breath with the grim focus of a man personally offended by fatigue.\n\nI leaned against the doorway.\n\n“Do you ever do anything casually?”\n\nHe lowered the bar.\n\n“You’re awake.”\n\n“Sharp observation.”\n\n“It’s 0500.”\n\n“That explains why I hate the world.”\n\nHe sat up and reached for a towel.\n\n“Physical readiness is part of my duty.”\n\n“You could be physically ready at seven.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Six-thirty?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Compromise means both people suffer.”\n\n“That is not what compromise means.”\n\nI stepped into the room and examined the collection of scavenged weights. Several were old engine parts threaded onto a steel pipe.\n\n“You made these.”\n\n“Rhys did.”\n\nThat surprised me enough that I looked toward the sleeping area.\n\nDanse noticed.\n\n“He is more resourceful than his disposition suggests.”\n\n“That is the kindest insult I’ve ever heard.”\n\n“It was not an insult.”\n\n“Of course not.”\n\nHe gave me the look I was beginning to collect.\n\nA few minutes later he showed me how to brace the improvised bar so it would not roll off its stand. Then he corrected my grip. Then my stance. Then, because he was Danse, he turned the entire interaction into a safety briefing.\n\n“You are taking this very seriously.”\n\n“Injury is preventable.”\n\n“So is boredom.”\n\n“Your priorities concern me.”\n\nBut he was smiling when he said it.\n\nAfterward we sat on the floor sharing a bottle of purified water.\n\nThe station was still quiet. Haylen’s transmitter clicked softly in the next room. Rhys snored with an aggression that seemed personally directed at sleep.\n\nDanse leaned his head back against the wall.\n\nFor once, no helmet. No rifle in his hands. No one asking him for a decision.\n\n“You look tired,” I said.\n\n“I’m fine.”\n\n“Your favorite lie.”\n\nHis eyes opened.\n\n“I don’t lie.”\n\n“You reorganized three ammunition crates at midnight.”\n\n“That needed to be done.”\n\n“They were already organized.”\n\n“Poorly.”\n\nI let the silence sit.\n\nEventually he looked away.\n\n“Sleep has been difficult since the expedition.”\n\nThe expedition.\n\nNot yet the names. Not yet the details.\n\nBut it was something.\n\nI passed him the water.\n\n“You don’t have to tell me.”\n\n“I know.”\n\nThat seemed to surprise him too.\n\nMost people, given a crack in armor, tried to pry it wider.\n\nI knew enough about damaged things to leave pressure off a fracture until it was ready to bear weight.\n\nDanse took another drink.\n\n“Thank you.”\n\n“For what?”\n\n“Not asking.”\n\nI shrugged.\n\n“When you want me to know, you’ll tell me.”\n\nHis eyes met mine.\n\nSomething in the room changed—not romance, not yet. Something quieter and more important.\n\nTrust did not always arrive as a confession.\n\nSometimes it arrived as the decision that one would be safe when it came.\n","word_count":1801},"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-19":{"number":19,"title":"Under the Tanks","content":"The first time Danse slept beside me, nothing happened.\n\nThat was the problem.\n\nA radstorm grounded all flights and kept half the Prydwen awake while green lightning flashed outside the hull.\n\nThe storm put the maintenance crew on a different schedule. Every time the airship lurched, boots hurried along the corridor outside my bunk and somebody called a pressure reading down to the next deck.\n\nDanse noticed the sound before I did.\n\n“That one's the stabilizer check,” he said after a metal clang. “And that's someone dropping a wrench.”\n\n“How can you tell?”\n\n“Because the wrench is followed by profanity. The stabilizer isn't.”\n\nI laughed despite myself. Then another gust knocked the cup off my shelf, and the laughter went out of me.\n\nDanse bent to pick it up. He didn't tell me there was nothing to fear. That would have been an insult to both of us. We were in the air, inside a ship with working machinery and tired people watching it through a storm.\n\n“I can ask the crew for a status report,” he said.\n\n“You don't have to order anyone down here.”\n\n“I wasn't going to.”\n\nHe stopped himself and gave me the smallest grimace. “I was considering it.”\n\nWe waited for the scheduled announcement. When it came, the captain reported the ship stable and all flights grounded until the radstorm passed. It was more useful than an invented reassurance.\n\n“Can you stay?” I asked.\n\n“For as long as you want company.”\n\n“Even if I don't sleep?”\n\nHe took off his boots and sat on the far edge of the bunk, leaving me room to choose how close to move.\n\n“Especially then,” he said. “I dislike the sound of your silence more than the ship.”\n\nI rested against his shoulder. His shirt smelled faintly of clean cotton and machine oil, and for a moment we were not two people waiting to be brave. We were two frightened adults who had agreed not to leave the other guessing. The airship shifted in the wind. Every metal sound became a reminder that we were suspended over Boston by engineering and collective confidence.\n\nI hated it.\n\nDanse discovered this at approximately 0100 when he found me sitting fully dressed on my bunk.\n\n“You’re awake.”\n\n“So are you.”\n\n“I’m on watch.”\n\n“Convenient.”\n\nA green flash lit the narrow quarters.\n\nThe ship creaked.\n\nMy hands tightened around the edge of the mattress.\n\nDanse noticed.\n\nHe always noticed.\n\n“Storm bothering you?”\n\n“No.”\n\nAnother impact of thunder.\n\nI flinched.\n\nHe folded his arms.\n\n“Your lying needs work.”\n\n“Your coffee needs work.”\n\n“Unrelated.”\n\n“Still true.”\n\nDanse looked around the sleeping bay. Most of the bunks were occupied. Someone snored nearby with the confidence of a person who had accepted death.\n\n“You want to walk?” he asked.\n\n“Where?”\n\n“Anywhere with fewer hydrogen tanks over your head.”\n\n“You admitted those were hydrogen?”\n\n“I said mostly.”\n\n“Danse.”\n\nHe smiled.\n\nWe ended up in a maintenance alcove with two folding chairs and a view through a small reinforced port.\n\nThe storm rolled over the city below. Green radiation lit ruined towers from within the clouds.\n\nDanse handed me Rad-X.\n\n“I already took some.”\n\n“Take another.”\n\n“That sounds medically irresponsible.”\n\n“I checked the dosage.”\n\nOf course he had.\n\nWe sat.\n\nThe wind shook the hull.\n\nI gripped the chair.\n\nDanse put his hand over mine.\n\nNo speech.\n\nNo question.\n\nJust pressure and warmth.\n\nMy fingers loosened.\n\n“You afraid of anything?” I asked.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nThe immediate answer surprised me.\n\n“What?”\n\nHe looked out the port.\n\n“Failing people who depend on me.”\n\n“I guessed that one.”\n\n“Losing control of a situation.”\n\n“Also guessed.”\n\nHe hesitated.\n\n“Being buried alive.”\n\nI turned toward him.\n\n“Really?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Collapsed building when I was younger. Scavenging.”\n\n“How long?”\n\n“Several hours.”\n\n“Alone?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nI squeezed his hand.\n\nHe looked down at our fingers.\n\n“Your turn.”\n\n“Storms on airships, apparently.”\n\n“That’s specific.”\n\n“Commitment to craft.”\n\n“What else?”\n\nI stared at the storm.\n\n“Depending on people.”\n\nDanse went still.\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Same reason as you, probably. If you depend on someone, they can disappear.”\n\nHe understood too quickly.\n\nThe conversation did not need details.\n\nWe sat holding hands while the Prydwen shook.\n\nAt some point my head rested against his shoulder.\n\nAt some point his cheek touched the top of my head.\n\nAt some point I stopped listening for every change in the engines.\n\nWhen the storm weakened, I should have gone back to my bunk.\n\nI did not.\n\nDanse should have returned to watch.\n\nHe did not.\n\nThe two folding chairs were terrible for sleeping. Eventually we gave up and returned to the quarters.\n\nMy bunk was narrow.\n\nDanse looked at it.\n\nThen at me.\n\n“This is a bad idea.”\n\n“Probably.”\n\n“Regulations—”\n\n“We’re sleeping.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“Fully clothed.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“You’re the one making it weird.”\n\n“I am not making it weird.”\n\n“Then lie down.”\n\nHe stared at me like I had ordered him to assault Maxson.\n\nFinally he removed his boots and lay on top of the blanket beside me.\n\nThere was barely room.\n\nHis shoulder pressed against the wall. I was half against his chest.\n\n“This is ridiculous,” he whispered.\n\n“Shh. Some of us are trying to sleep.”\n\n“You started this.”\n\n“Good night, Paladin.”\n\nA long pause.\n\nThen his arm settled carefully around me.\n\nNot possessive.\n\nProtective almost by habit, except there was nothing to protect me from except the storm.\n\nMaybe that was enough.\n\nI could feel his heartbeat beneath my cheek.\n\nSteady.\n\nHuman, my mind supplied automatically.\n\nI did not know why the thought came with emphasis.\n\nDanse’s fingers rested against my shoulder.\n\n“Comfortable?” he asked.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Good.”\n\nMinutes passed.\n\nHis breathing slowed.\n\nThe storm moved east.\n\nNothing happened.\n\nNo kiss. No confession. We slept, sharing the quiet without asking the night to become anything more.\n\nIn the morning I woke to Danse already awake, staring at the ceiling with the expression of a man who had discovered a tactical error with no approved procedure.\n\n“Morning,” I whispered.\n\nHe looked down at me.\n\n“Morning.”\n\nNeither of us moved.\n\nSomeone coughed from the next row of bunks.\n\nDanse’s eyes widened.\n\nI bit my lip.\n\n“Don’t laugh.”\n\n“I’m not.”\n\n“You are.”\n\n“I’m asleep.”\n\nHe extracted himself with impossible care and put on his boots.\n\nAt the door he paused.\n\n“For the record…”\n\n“Yes?”\n\nHe looked profoundly uncomfortable.\n\n“I slept well.”\n\nMy chest warmed.\n\n“Me too.”\n\nDanse nodded and left.\n\nFive minutes later Haylen walked past my bunk, glanced at the empty space beside me, and smiled without saying a word.\n\nI decided silence could be an act of violence.\n\nThe second time he slept beside me was deliberate.\n\nThat made it more frightening.\n\nA radstorm rolled over Cambridge after midnight, turning the windows green and making the old police station groan in its frame. I was awake counting seconds between thunder when Danse appeared beside my bunk.\n\nHe wore fatigues and socks.\n\nThe socks somehow made the entire situation more intimate.\n\n“Can’t sleep?” I asked.\n\n“No.”\n\n“Nightmares?”\n\nA pause.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHe did not elaborate.\n\nI moved over.\n\nDanse looked at the space.\n\n“You don’t have to—”\n\n“Get in before Rhys wakes up and turns this into a military incident.”\n\nThat got him moving.\n\nThe bunk was not built for two people, especially when one of them was Danse. We arranged ourselves with the logistical seriousness of planning an assault.\n\n“Your elbow is in my ribs.”\n\n“Your knee is occupying most of the lower mattress.”\n\n“I have two knees.”\n\n“I’m aware.”\n\n“Then be grateful only one is a problem.”\n\nHe made a quiet choking sound.\n\n“Was that a laugh?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Coward.”\n\nEventually we settled facing each other.\n\nLightning flickered through the curtains.\n\nDanse’s face was close enough that I could see the shadow beneath his eyes.\n\n“What happens in the dreams?” I asked.\n\nHis gaze went to the ceiling.\n\n“Different things.”\n\n“Useful.”\n\n“Sometimes Gladius.”\n\nHis voice dropped.\n\n“Sometimes Cutler.”\n\nI waited.\n\n“Sometimes I arrive too late. Sometimes I’m there in time and it changes nothing.”\n\n“That sounds worse.”\n\n“It is.”\n\nThunder rolled over the station.\n\nDanse’s hand lay between us, open on the blanket.\n\nI placed mine beside it, not touching.\n\nHe closed the distance.\n\n“I don’t understand why the mind does that,” he said. “Reconstructs failures with new details as if repetition can produce a different result.”\n\n“Maybe it’s trying to solve something that can’t be solved.”\n\n“That’s inefficient.”\n\n“The brain did not consult the Brotherhood manual.”\n\n“Clearly.”\n\nHis thumb moved over the side of my hand.\n\nThe storm flashed again.\n\n“You make it quieter,” he said.\n\nI almost asked what he meant.\n\nThen I understood.\n\nNot the weather.\n\nI swallowed.\n\n“So do you.”\n\nDanse looked back at me.\n\nFor a moment I thought he might kiss me.\n\nHe did not.\n\nInstead he shifted closer until his forehead rested against mine.\n\nThe contact was so careful it hurt more than a kiss probably would have.\n\nWe stayed that way through another roll of thunder.\n\nEventually his breathing slowed.\n\nMine followed.\n\nI woke before dawn with Danse asleep on his side, one hand curled loosely into my shirt as if sometime during the night he had needed proof I was still there.\n\nI did not move until he woke on his own.\n\nWhen his eyes opened, confusion lasted half a second.\n\nSometime before dawn the storm became rain.\n\nThe sound changed from violent impacts against the hull to a steady metallic tapping. Danse stirred but did not wake.\n\nWithout the power armor and uniform, sleep made him look younger. Less like a paladin and more like the scrap trader he had once described in fragments: a man from Rivet City who had learned to sleep lightly because possessions vanished and trouble did not announce itself.\n\nHis grip tightened once in my shirt.\n\nI thought about waking him.\n\nInstead I rested my hand over his.\n\nThe tension in his fingers eased.\n\nIt struck me then that Danse’s idea of safety had always involved being the person awake enough to provide it. Guard duty. Perimeter checks. Armor between danger and everyone else.\n\nHe did not know what to do with safety offered back.\n\nMaybe this was the smallest version of it.\n\nA storm passing.\n\nA bunk too narrow for two people.\n\nSomeone still there when he opened his eyes.\n\nThen memory returned.\n\nHis hand released my shirt immediately.\n\n“Apologies.”\n\n“No.”\n\nHe blinked.\n\n“No apologizing for sleeping.”\n\n“That was not—”\n\n“Or for holding onto me.”\n\nHis face went red.\n\nI smiled.\n\n“Good morning.”\n\nDanse stared at me, helpless for once.\n\nThen he sighed.\n\n“Good morning.”\n\nWe ate breakfast in near silence.\n\nDanse attempted to behave as if waking with his hand twisted in my shirt had been an ordinary logistical event.\n\nI allowed this fiction until he reached for the salt and knocked over his own cup.\n\nHe stared at the spreading coffee.\n\nI stared at him.\n\n“Say nothing.”\n\n“I haven’t.”\n\n“Your face is saying plenty.”\n\n“Operationally?”\n\nHe closed his eyes.\n\nI cleaned the spill before he could turn embarrassment into retreat. When I handed him the rag, our fingers touched.\n\nThis time he left them there for one extra second.\n\nOutside, the radstorm had passed.\n\nInside, something else had not.","word_count":1861},"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-36":{"number":36,"title":"Three Chords","content":"Danse learned three chords.\n\nIt took him two months.\n\nNot because the chords were difficult.\n\nBecause every time he made a mistake, he tried to turn music practice into military discipline.\n\n“Again,” he muttered.\n\nThe chord buzzed badly.\n\n“Relax your hand.”\n\n“I am relaxed.”\n\n“You’re gripping the neck like it insulted Maxson.”\n\nHis fingers loosened.\n\n“Again.”\n\n“See? You do listen to me.”\n\n“Occasionally.”\n\nWe sat on the porch of the rebuilt house. The borrowed guitar had become a permanent gift after the settlement musician found another instrument.\n\nDanse had resisted accepting it.\n\nThen spent six hours cleaning it.\n\nNow he played the first chord.\n\nThen the second.\n\nThen the third.\n\nAll clean.\n\nHe stopped.\n\nI grinned.\n\nDanse looked at his fingers.\n\n“Again.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“What?”\n\n“You did it.”\n\n“I can improve the transition speed.”\n\n“Tomorrow.”\n\nHe frowned.\n\n“I have momentum.”\n\n“You have a successful ending. Learn to recognize one.”\n\nThat landed deeper than I intended.\n\nDanse looked out over the road.\n\nA caravan was moving in the distance. Sunlight warmed the dead grass. Somewhere behind the house, our new windmill squeaked every third rotation.\n\n“I don’t know how,” he admitted.\n\n“What?”\n\n“Recognize an ending before something goes wrong.”\n\nI sat beside him.\n\n“You’re waiting for the next casualty.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“The next order.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“The next revelation that destroys your life.”\n\nHe gave me a look.\n\n“Dramatic.”\n\n“Accurate, though.”\n\nHis mouth twitched.\n\nI touched the guitar.\n\n“Three chords. Nothing went wrong.”\n\n“Yet.”\n\n“Danse.”\n\nHe exhaled.\n\n“Three chords,” he said.\n\n“Good.”\n\n“Successful objective.”\n\n“Not an objective.”\n\n“Successful…”\n\nHe searched.\n\n“Thing.”\n\nI laughed.\n\n“Perfect.”\n\nHe set the guitar aside.\n\nThen pulled me into his lap with enough ease to remind me that civilian clothes had not changed how strong he was.\n\nI made a surprised noise.\n\nDanse looked pleased.\n\n“That was deliberate.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“Just clarifying.”\n\nHis arms settled around me.\n\nWe watched the sunset.\n\nAfter a while I asked, “Do you miss the armor?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Do you miss being a paladin?”\n\nA longer pause.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nI waited.\n\n“I don’t miss what I believed I had to be.”\n\nI turned toward him.\n\n“What was that?”\n\n“Certain.”\n\nThe answer surprised me.\n\nDanse looked down at his hands.\n\n“I thought doubt was weakness. Commanders can’t hesitate. Soldiers can’t question every threat. You make decisions and live with them.”\n\n“That part is still true.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“But?”\n\n“But certainty became easier than thinking.”\n\nThat was the closest he had come to naming the thing that had almost killed him.\n\nI kissed his cheek.\n\n“Three chords.”\n\nHe smiled.\n\n“Three chords.”\n\nThe next day he learned a fourth.\n\nThe fourth chord created a problem.\n\nThree chords had been an experiment.\n\nFour meant Danse now believed he should be able to play a song.\n\nThis was how I woke one morning to the same six seconds of music repeated from the porch with increasing hostility.\n\nI pulled on a shirt and opened the door.\n\nDanse sat in the early light with the guitar balanced on one knee, red scarf looped loosely around his neck because the morning was cold.\n\nHe played the progression.\n\nStopped.\n\nPlayed it again.\n\nStopped.\n\n“Are you fighting the song?” I asked.\n\n“No.”\n\n“You look like you’re fighting the song.”\n\n“The transition is too slow.”\n\n“It is six in the morning.”\n\n“That is unrelated.”\n\n“Nothing musical should happen at six in the morning unless somebody is being paid.”\n\nDanse looked at me.\n\n“You’re awake now.”\n\n“Against my will.”\n\nI sat beside him anyway.\n\nHe played again.\n\nThis time I recognized the shape of an old pre-War tune we had heard on a trader’s radio.\n\n“You’ve been practicing in secret.”\n\n“I practice while you’re doing supply runs.”\n\n“Why?”\n\nHis fingers stopped.\n\nThe answer took long enough that I knew it mattered.\n\n“I wanted to surprise you.”\n\nThat was almost offensively sweet.\n\nI leaned my shoulder against his.\n\n“You did.”\n\n“It isn’t ready.”\n\n“I didn’t say it was good.”\n\nHe looked wounded.\n\nI laughed and kissed the corner of his jaw.\n\n“It’s getting there.”\n\nDanse stared at the strings.\n\n“I don’t know why I care whether it sounds good to you.”\n\n“Yes, you do.”\n\n“Fine. I don’t know why I care this much.”\n\n“That I believe.”\n\nHe set the guitar aside.\n\n“When I was in the Brotherhood, praise was measurable. Accuracy scores. completed objectives. promotions. A superior told you whether you met the standard.”\n\n“And now?”\n\n“Now I play four chords on a porch and wait for your face to tell me whether it meant anything.”\n\nI turned toward him.\n\n“That sounds terrifying.”\n\n“It is.”\n\n“Good.”\n\nHis eyes narrowed.\n\n“We have discussed your inappropriate use of that word.”\n\n“I mean it’s vulnerable.”\n\n“That does not make it good.”\n\n“No. But you’re doing it anyway.”\n\nHe looked toward the road.\n\nThe windmill squeaked.\n\nA crow landed on the fence and immediately reconsidered the location when Danse picked the guitar up again.\n\n“This bird has better judgment than the dog,” I said.\n\n“Quiet.”\n\nHe began the progression.\n\nThis time he did not stop when one chord buzzed.\n\nHe kept going.\n\nThe song remained imperfect. The rhythm dragged. One transition arrived late enough that I could practically see him wanting to restart.\n\nHe did not.\n\nHe reached the end.\n\nThen looked at me.\n\nI clapped once.\n\n“Do not be patronizing.”\n\nI clapped louder.\n\nDanse tried not to smile.\n\nFailed.\n\n“You finished it,” I said.\n\n“I made three errors.”\n\n“And finished it.”\n\n“The timing—”\n\n“Finished.”\n\nHe breathed out.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nThat afternoon the settlement held a small market. We brought repaired weapons, spare parts, and the guitar because one of the children had asked whether Danse would play.\n\nHe nearly refused.\n\nThen looked at me.\n\nI raised both hands.\n\n“Your choice.”\n\nHe hated that answer.\n\nHe played anyway.\n\nTen people listened. Nobody cared about the late chord. A little girl danced in circles with Captain the dog until both fell over.\n\nWhen Danse finished, the workshop owner cheered loudest.\n\nHe looked embarrassed.\n\nThen happy.\n\nNot proud because he had been best.\n\nProud because he had shared something unfinished and the world had not punished him for it.\n\nThe settlement asked him to play again the following week.\n\nDanse assumed they were being polite.\n\n“They heard the first performance,” he said. “They have sufficient information.”\n\n“Maybe they liked it.”\n\n“That would indicate poor judgment.”\n\nHe played anyway.\n\nThis time he made it through the first verse before missing the transition. His hand stopped automatically.\n\nNobody booed.\n\nA child near the fire shouted, “Keep going!”\n\nDanse looked at me.\n\nI lifted both hands. “Not an order.”\n\nHe looked back at the strings.\n\nThen continued from the next chord instead of restarting.\n\nThe mistake remained inside the song.\n\nSo did the song.\n\nWhen he finished, the applause was louder than before.\n\nDanse sat very still.\n\nLater an old farmer came over and said, “My wife used to love that tune.”\n\n“I played it badly,” Danse replied.\n\nThe farmer shrugged. “Still knew what it was.”\n\nDanse had no answer.\n\nOn the porch afterward, he told me that sentence bothered him.\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because I keep assuming imperfect means unrecognizable.”\n\nI leaned against his shoulder.\n\n“And?”\n\n“And apparently that’s not always true.”\n\nHe played the troublesome transition once more.\n\nIt buzzed.\n\nHe did not start over.\n\nOne of the children from the settlement came over with a cheap instrument missing a tuning peg.\n\n“Can you fix this?” she asked Danse.\n\nHe looked at the damaged headstock as though she'd presented him with a malfunctioning weapon.\n\n“I can examine it,” he said. “I cannot guarantee a repair without the correct part.”\n\n“That's okay.”\n\nHe laid out a cloth to protect the finish and studied the fracture. His fingers were patient; his voice still had the roughness of a man accustomed to giving instructions in dangerous places.\n\n“You could ask Greg,” I said. “He knows instruments.”\n\nThe next time Greg came through the Starwake Door, he brought a soft case full of spare strings and an expression of delighted concern.\n\n“Hey, man. Heard there's a guitar emergency.”\n\nDanse stood on the porch like a soldier answering a formal summons. “My technique is inadequate.”\n\nGreg blinked. “Wow. Usually people just say their fingers hurt.”\n\n“They do.”\n\n“Great. Then we're already speaking the same language.”\n\nI tried very hard not to laugh. Greg sat on the porch step and took the guitar when Danse offered it. He didn't snatch it away or play a complicated solo to demonstrate what a real musician could do. He checked the tuning, let Danse see the worn fret, and pointed out why one string was buzzing even when the chord shape was right.\n\n“Some of it's your hand,” Greg said. “Some of it's this poor old guitar asking for a little help.”\n\n“Then the fault is not entirely mine.”\n\n“Man, I wish you could tell that to my first manager.”\n\nDanse looked at him. “Was your manager incompetent?”\n\n“That's a longer song.”\n\nDanse took the instrument back. He played the chord slowly, adjusting his grip in small increments. This time the notes sounded clear.\n\n“You're a good teacher,” I told Greg.\n\n“Hey, I just know what it feels like when a piece of equipment makes you think you're terrible at the thing you love.”\n\nGreg had to get back for a sound check. Danse thanked him with a formality that made Greg almost drop the case.\n\nAfter he left, Danse touched the strings with one finger.\n\n“He teaches differently from you,” he said.\n\n“I'm not a guitarist.”\n\n“No. You allow me to be stubborn for longer.”\n\n“I enjoy the entertainment.”\n\nHe looked down, then back up with a rare, unguarded smile. For once he didn't make the smile something to be measured.\n\n“Then I should ask him,” Danse replied. “Not invent expertise because someone expects me to be useful.”\n\nThere was a time when he would have treated admitting a limit as failure. Now he wrote down the part the child needed and asked her who actually owned the guitar. It was her mother's, she explained. They agreed to return it until the proper peg could be found.\n\n“You didn't fix it,” I told him when she left.\n\n“No.”\n\n“You helped.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHe considered that, then laughed once, almost surprised by the sound.\n\n“Apparently there are successful outcomes that do not involve completing the task myself.”\n\nI raised an eyebrow. “Careful. That's dangerously close to a philosophy.”\n\n“Then strike it from the record,” he said.\n\nHe reached for the guitar case. The child had not given him a medal or a new order. She'd trusted him with a small problem, and he'd been careful enough not to make it larger.\n\n\nAfter the applause, Danse packed the guitar as carefully as he once packed a laser rifle.\n\nThe old farmer stopped him again.\n\n“Come by next week,” the man said. “I know another song you might like.”\n\nDanse’s first instinct was visible: refuse before obligation could form.\n\nThen he glanced at me.\n\nNot for permission.\n\nFor orientation.\n\n“I might,” he said.\n\nThe farmer nodded as if *might* were enough.\n\nOn the road, I bumped my shoulder against his.\n\n“Look at you making social plans.”\n\n“I made no commitment.”\n\n“You said might.”\n\n“That is explicitly noncommittal.”\n\n“For you, that’s practically spontaneous.”\n\nHe muttered something about inaccurate standards.\n\nBut his hand found mine anyway.\n\nHalfway down the road he played the troublesome transition while walking.\n\nIt was worse than before.\n\nHe laughed first.\n\nDanse looked back once toward the fire, where someone else had started another song. He did not look embarrassed anymore. Mostly, he looked as though he wanted to remember the sound.\n\nThe strings had left shallow marks across his fingertips. He studied them with absurd satisfaction, like evidence that the afternoon had actually happened.\n\n“Worth it?” I asked.\n\n“Yes,” he said, and did not qualify the answer.\n\nOn the walk home, he carried the guitar over one shoulder.\n\n“You know what the worst part is?” he asked.\n\n“What?”\n\n“I want to learn the whole song.”\n\nI gasped theatrically.\n\n“Ambition.”\n\n“And another after that.”\n\n“Dangerous.”\n\n“I may even learn one you like.”\n\nI took his free hand.\n\n“I already like this one.”\n\n“It was badly played.”\n\n“That wasn’t what I said.”\n\nDanse stopped.\n\nHe recognized the sentence.\n\nOne of his, from the beginning.\n\nThe smile came slowly.\n\n“Fair point.”\n\nWe kept walking.\n\nBehind us the settlement noise faded.\n\nAhead, our house waited with a porch large enough for two people, one guitar, and as many imperfect songs as he wanted to learn.\n","word_count":2082}},"after-always":{"after-always-06":{"number":6,"title":"A Question of Antidotes","content":"The sixth hidden phial tried to bite me.\n\nBefore the glass could strike again, Severus pulled a heavy ledger from beneath the bench. Its corners were scarred by years of impatient handling. He compared the ink on the vial's outer label with a notation in the ledger and made a displeased sound.\n\n“What?”\n\n“The mixture has been catalogued under a name it has never had. Whoever wrote this assumes that two red reagents with similar behavior are interchangeable.”\n\n“They aren't?”\n\n“If they were, we would not need to bother studying them.”\n\nHe turned the page so I could see the old formula. Part of the entry was crossed through, not with a dramatic warning but with the blunt word *unverified*. Beneath it he'd listed three things he hadn't been able to reproduce.\n\n“You kept the mistakes,” I said.\n\n“I kept the observations. The failures are more useful than the account of a perfect result that never occurred.”\n\nI held the shield while he inspected the seal. The potion's little red mouth opened against the glass, soundless and ugly. When I moved the silver spoon too close, he stopped me with a sharp, immediate instruction.\n\n“Back.”\n\nI obeyed. After the reaction subsided, he lowered his wand.\n\n“That was a safety instruction,” he said. “Not an invitation to prove you trust me by taking a risk.”\n\n“Good. Because my trust doesn't come with a willingness to be bitten by laboratory equipment.”\n\nHis mouth twitched. He wrote down the time, the temperature and the angle of the spoon. The unknown remained unknown; at least it had stopped pretending to be familiar.\n\nNot metaphorically.\n\nThe moment I broke the outer seal, the potion rose from the glass in a narrow red strand, formed something remarkably like a mouth, and snapped at the silver testing spoon.\n\nI jerked my hand back.\n\nSnape did not.\n\nHis wand moved once.\n\nThe red strand froze in midair, shivered, and dropped back into the phial.\n\n“Impressive,” I said.\n\n“Careless.”\n\n“I meant the potion.”\n\n“So did I.”\n\nI looked at him.\n\nHe had already turned away, reaching for a containment bell.\n\n“Did you know it would do that?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“You don't sound surprised.”\n\n“I have found surprise to be of limited practical value.”\n\n“That explains your parties.”\n\n“I do not attend parties.”\n\n“That explains them even more.”\n\nThe corner of his mouth threatened movement.\n\nHe placed the glass bell over the phial and sealed it with three flicks of his wand. The potion struck the inside of the dome once, then settled.\n\n“Blood-reactive,” he said.\n\n“I didn't cut myself.”\n\n“No, but you handled the bitterroot earlier.”\n\nI looked at my fingertips. “Trace residue?”\n\n“Possibly.”\n\n“That is extraordinarily sensitive.”\n\n“It was designed to be.”\n\n“For what?”\n\nHis expression changed.\n\nThere were moments when Snape became so still that I could almost see the decision happening behind his eyes: how much truth, how much omission, how much protection.\n\n“Interrogation,” he said.\n\nThe word landed heavily.\n\n“The draught was intended to react to elevated pulse and blood chemistry. Fear, pain, exertion. It would alter color when a subject became sufficiently distressed.”\n\n“A lie detector?”\n\n“A crude one.”\n\n“Voldemort wanted it?”\n\n“One of his lieutenants did. The Dark Lord approved.”\n\nI looked at the phial.\n\n“What did you do to it?”\n\nSnape's gaze sharpened.\n\n“You assume I did something.”\n\n“The other five were sabotaged.”\n\n“Patterns can encourage laziness.”\n\n“So can evasiveness.”\n\nHe ignored that.\n\nI ran the residual magic reader along the bell. It chimed twice, then shrieked.\n\n“Interesting.”\n\n“Move.”\n\nSnape reached past me.\n\nI caught his wrist before his fingers touched the bell.\n\nWe both froze.\n\nRule: no touching without warning unless someone was falling.\n\nApparently I had discovered an amendment for someone about to touch a blood-reactive potion.\n\nHis skin was cool. His pulse was not.\n\n“Don't,” I said.\n\nHis eyes dropped to my hand.\n\nI released him at once.\n\n“What did the reader show?” he asked.\n\n“Two layers. One old, one recent.”\n\n“Recent?”\n\n“Within months.”\n\n“That is impossible.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because I brewed it years ago.”\n\nWe looked at each other.\n\nThen at the phial.\n\nSomeone had tampered with it after Snape.\n\nThe room changed around us.\n\nUntil then, the audit had been archaeology. Dangerous archaeology, but still the sorting of a finished war.\n\nNow the past had a fresh fingerprint.\n\nSnape drew his wand.\n\nThis time I did too.\n\n“Who had access?” I asked.\n\n“During the last school year? The Carrows. Selected Death Eaters. Anyone Voldemort chose to send.”\n\n“After the battle?”\n\n“Ministry evidence teams.”\n\n“Reconstruction staff?”\n\n“Not to the sealed stores.”\n\nI thought of the wax. The official stamps. The careless question mark on the cabinet.\n\n“Unless someone broke a seal and replaced it.”\n\nSnape's face became unpleasantly focused.\n\n“Your instrument.”\n\nI handed it to him.\n\nHe scanned the phial, then the crate, then the shelf where we had found it. The needle quivered near the back wall.\n\n“Again,” he said.\n\nI joined him.\n\nWe moved every jar.\n\nBehind the shelf was a narrow crack in the stone.\n\nSnape pressed his wand tip against it and whispered a spell I did not recognize.\n\nThe mortar glowed green.\n\n“Concealment charm,” he said.\n\n“Yours?”\n\n“No.”\n\nThe wall opened.\n\nInside was a cavity no larger than a bread box.\n\nIt held four more phials.\n\nNone were his.\n\nI knew because he swore.\n\nIt was the first time I had heard him do it.\n\nNot loudly. Not creatively. But with enough feeling that I almost laughed before remembering what we were looking at.\n\nEach phial bore a thin silver mark etched into the glass: a hooked A.\n\n“Alecto?” I asked.\n\nHis jaw tightened.\n\n“Possibly.”\n\n“What's in them?”\n\n“I do not know.”\n\nThe admission came reluctantly.\n\nI liked it more than I should have.\n\nSnape was frightening when he knew everything in a room. There was something unexpectedly reassuring about hearing him say he did not.\n\n“Then we find out,” I said.\n\n“No.”\n\nI blinked.\n\n“No?”\n\n“You will find out from behind a shield.”\n\n“And you?”\n\n“I will handle the samples.”\n\n“You're still recovering from venom and nerve damage.”\n\n“I am also more familiar with Dark potions than you are.”\n\n“That does not make you poison-proof.”\n\n“It does make me less likely to kill us through ignorance.”\n\nI folded my arms.\n\nHe gave me a look that had probably terrorized adolescents for decades.\n\nIt did not improve with age.\n\n“No,” I said.\n\nHis voice dropped. “This is not a negotiation.”\n\n“Everything between adults is a negotiation unless someone is using force.”\n\nHis expression changed at that.\n\nI continued before he could decide whether to be angry.\n\n“We work together. You identify likely hazards. I handle precision work when your hand tires. We both stay behind the shield whenever the phials are open.”\n\n“I do not require assistance.”\n\n“You nearly dropped a memory two days ago.”\n\nHis face went white with anger.\n\nI regretted the cruelty immediately, but not the point.\n\n“I'm not saying you're incapable,” I said. “I'm saying your body is still healing, and pretending otherwise does not make you safer. It makes you easier to kill.”\n\nSilence.\n\nHis gaze went to the hidden compartment.\n\nWhen he spoke, the anger had become something colder.\n\n“I have been easy to kill for some time.”\n\n“No,” I said. “You've been willing to die. That's different.”\n\nHis eyes snapped to mine.\n\nThe words had come too fast to soften.\n\nI could have apologized.\n\nI did not.\n\nFor once, I wanted him to hear me without escape.\n\nHis throat worked.\n\n“Do not presume—”\n\n“I don't presume anything about why. I'm looking at the formula you wrote. Seven days of tissue saturation. Multiple neutralizers. A warning not to rely on it alone. You prepared to survive and then behaved as if survival was an administrative error.”\n\nHis face was unreadable.\n\nMine probably wasn't.\n\n“That is not professional analysis,” he said.\n\n“No.”\n\n“What is it?”\n\nI had no safe answer.\n\nConcern sounded too intimate.\n\nFrustration sounded too small.\n\n“I don't know yet,” I said.\n\nFor some reason, honesty unsettled him more than either would have.\n\nHe looked away first.\n\n“Shield,” he said.\n\nI exhaled.\n\n“Together?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nIt was as close to surrender as Severus Snape seemed capable of giving.\n\nThe first of Alecto's phials contained a modified Befuddlement Draught with a pain catalyst. The second contained a corrosive suspension meant to scar without immediately disabling. The third was a concentration of something I had never seen outside restricted medical texts.\n\nSnape recognized it.\n\n“Stop.”\n\nI froze.\n\nHe leaned close to the glass, eyes narrowing.\n\n“Acromantula venom.”\n\n“Stabilized?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“With what?”\n\nHe stared.\n\nThen he smiled.\n\nIt was not a pleasant smile.\n\n“Poorly.”\n\nHe pointed at a faint sediment along the bottom.\n\n“The stabilizer is separating. Another month and this would have become inert.”\n\n“Deliberate?”\n\n“No. Incompetence.”\n\n“You sound delighted.”\n\n“I am capable of enjoying small mercies.”\n\n“Particularly when they insult a Carrow.”\n\n“Especially then.”\n\nWe documented everything.\n\nBy evening, we had enough evidence to prove that the hidden cache was not Snape's and that at least one potion had been altered recently.\n\nMcGonagall came down herself.\n\nHer face hardened as she read the report.\n\n“This goes to Kingsley tonight.”\n\nSnape's expression shuttered at the Minister's name.\n\n“He will require a statement,” she added.\n\n“I have given statements.”\n\n“You have given insults in the approximate shape of statements.”\n\n“They were accurate.”\n\nMcGonagall looked at me.\n\nI looked at the ceiling.\n\n“I shall send an Auror tomorrow,” she said.\n\nSnape's hand tightened on his cane.\n\n“No.”\n\n“Severus.”\n\n“No Aurors in my laboratory.”\n\n“It is not currently your laboratory.”\n\nThe words struck harder than she meant them to.\n\nI saw it.\n\nMcGonagall saw it too.\n\nHer mouth softened, but Snape had already turned away.\n\n“Use my office,” I said.\n\nBoth of them looked at me.\n\n“I don't have an office,” I clarified. “So we can invent one. Empty classroom, neutral ground, whatever makes everyone stop posturing long enough to exchange information.”\n\nMcGonagall's eyebrows rose.\n\nSnape looked murderous.\n\n“Tomorrow at ten,” she said.\n\nThen she left before either of us could object.\n\nI began packing instruments.\n\nSnape said, “You have an extraordinary talent for making my life worse.”\n\n“You were supposed to be dead. There's significant room for improvement.”\n\nHe stared at me.\n\nThen, impossibly, he laughed.\n\nIt hurt him. He pressed a hand to his throat and scowled as if the pain were my fault.\n\nI smiled anyway.\n\nOn the table between us, the blood-reactive potion snapped once more at the glass.\n\nThe past was not finished.\n\nNeither, apparently, was he.","word_count":1758},"after-always-19":{"number":19,"title":"The Apology He Hates","content":"It turned out Severus Snape could flirt.\n\nIt took me longer to recognize his version of courtship than I wanted to admit.\n\nThe first offering was a replacement quill, better balanced than mine and fitted for the way I held my hand. He left it beside my notes without a word. The second was a clean copy of a reference he had told me was “insultingly imprecise” two days earlier, now annotated in his narrow handwriting.\n\nThe third was an invitation to argue.\n\n“Your conclusion regarding the antidote is weak,” he announced over breakfast.\n\n“Good morning to you too.”\n\n“The source you cite treats the delayed effect as irrelevant. It is anything but.”\n\n“Did you actually read my paper?”\n\n“Unfortunately. Several times.”\n\nHe looked down at his tea before I could see his expression. I would have enjoyed teasing him all morning, but the letter from prison had made him jump at sudden noises. There were things that couldn't be charmed away with cleverness.\n\nI placed my hand palm-up on the table. He studied it as if it were a questionable ingredient.\n\n“Not a demand,” I said. “Just an offer.”\n\n“Your habit of offering things and expecting no receipt is irritating.”\n\n“That's not a no.”\n\nHe set two fingers against mine. The contact was so slight I barely felt it, yet he held it long enough for me to stop thinking about the letter on the desk. Then he withdrew to finish his tea.\n\nWe still needed to discuss the threat, the research and the boundary he had asked for. His hand had not solved any of those problems. It had told me he wanted to return to the conversation when he was ready.\n\nHe was simply malicious about it.\n\nFor three days after Hogsmeade, he behaved as though nothing had changed while changing everything by half an inch.\n\nHe stood closer when reading over my shoulder.\n\nHe passed ingredients directly into my hand instead of setting them down.\n\nHe said “good morning” before insulting my filing system.\n\nOnce, while I was bent over a lower cabinet, he murmured, “Your observational skills appear compromised from that angle.”\n\nI hit my head on the shelf.\n\nHe looked unbearably pleased.\n\n“This is your fault,” I said, rubbing my scalp.\n\n“Your spatial awareness is not my responsibility.”\n\n“You distracted me.”\n\n“With a factual statement.”\n\n“You're awful.”\n\n“And yet.”\n\nAnd yet.\n\nTwo words nearly undid me.\n\nThe problem with warmth from Snape was that it came in such small amounts I noticed every degree.\n\nWhich meant I also noticed when it vanished.\n\nThe letter arrived after lunch.\n\nNo official seal. No signature on the outside. Snape recognized the handwriting and went pale.\n\nHe opened it.\n\nRead three lines.\n\nFolded it.\n\nThen became impossible.\n\nNot ordinary impossible.\n\nSharp.\n\nHe criticized everything I touched. My knife angle. My labels. My pronunciation of an obscure ingredient name. When I asked if he wanted to stop work for the day, he accused me of condescension.\n\nI tolerated it for an hour.\n\nThen he said, “If your concentration is so easily disrupted by personal curiosity, perhaps you are unsuited to professional work.”\n\nI set down the jar.\n\n“No.”\n\nHe looked up.\n\n“You do not get to do that.”\n\n“Do what?”\n\n“Turn whatever is in that letter into an excuse to make me bleed.”\n\nHis face changed.\n\n“How melodramatic.”\n\n“There. That.”\n\n“You are imagining injury because you dislike criticism.”\n\n“I can take criticism. You've been criticizing me since we met. This is different.”\n\nHis eyes went cold.\n\n“You flatter yourself.”\n\nThe words hit harder than they should have.\n\nHe saw it.\n\nAnd then, because fear makes some people reach and others strike, Snape chose the blade.\n\n“You mistake proximity for importance,” he said.\n\nSilence.\n\nI felt something inside me go very quiet.\n\n“All right,” I said.\n\nHis face flickered.\n\nI took off my dragon-hide gloves and set them on the table.\n\n“You're right about one thing. I am too distracted to work safely.”\n\nI packed my notes.\n\nSnape watched.\n\n“What are you doing?”\n\n“Leaving.”\n\n“You are contracted until—”\n\n“For the day.”\n\nHis mouth tightened.\n\n“I did not dismiss you.”\n\n“I don't require dismissal.”\n\nThat landed.\n\nGood.\n\nI hated wanting it to.\n\nI left before he could say anything else.\n\nThe corridor blurred for the first several minutes, which I blamed on torch smoke because I had pride.\n\nI spent the afternoon in the library reviewing contamination law and understanding none of it.\n\nAt dinner, Snape did not appear.\n\nAt eight, someone knocked on my door.\n\nI knew who it was before opening.\n\nHe stood in the corridor without robes, wearing the same black coat from Spinner's End. His hair was damp. He held no umbrella.\n\nI folded my arms.\n\n“What?”\n\nSnape's face looked miserable.\n\nGood, a mean part of me thought.\n\nThen I hated that too.\n\n“I owe you an apology.”\n\nThe words sounded physically painful.\n\nI said nothing.\n\nHe continued.\n\n“What I said was cruel.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“It was also false.”\n\nMy chest tightened.\n\nHe looked at the floor for one second, then forced himself to meet my eyes.\n\n“You do not mistake proximity for importance.”\n\nI waited.\n\nSnape's jaw flexed.\n\n“You are important.”\n\nThere.\n\nNo poetry.\n\nNo softness around the edges.\n\nJust truth extracted at cost.\n\nI could have rescued him from the moment.\n\nI did not.\n\n“And?” I asked.\n\nHis eyes flashed with irritation.\n\nI almost smiled.\n\n“And I used knowledge of your… regard for me as a weapon because I was angry about something unrelated.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“That was contemptible.”\n\n“It was.”\n\n“You need not agree so enthusiastically.”\n\n“You need not be so accurate.”\n\nHis mouth twitched.\n\nNot enough.\n\nI leaned against the doorframe.\n\n“Why did the letter upset you?”\n\nHis face closed.\n\nI held up a hand.\n\n“You don't have to tell me. But if you're asking me to trust that this won't happen every time something scares you, I need to know you understand what happened.”\n\nA long silence.\n\n“The letter was from Lucius Malfoy.”\n\nThat I had not expected.\n\n“From prison?”\n\n“Awaiting formal sentencing.”\n\n“What did he want?”\n\n“To remind me of several things we did together.”\n\nThe words were flat.\n\n“To threaten you?”\n\n“No. Worse.”\n\nI waited.\n\n“To forgive me.”\n\nI stared.\n\nSnape laughed once without humor.\n\n“He wrote that we had both been used by a madman. That our mistakes should remain buried with him. That old friends ought not testify against one another.”\n\n“Ah.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“You're testifying.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“And he knows it.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nI understood now.\n\nLucius had reached through years of complicity and offered Snape the easiest lie: none of it was truly ours.\n\nNo wonder he had been furious.\n\nNo wonder he had turned it on himself.\n\nIt still did not excuse what he did to me.\n\nBoth can be true.\n\n“Did you answer?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Will you?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“What will you say?”\n\n“That he may call his choices whatever he likes. I will call mine mine.”\n\nI exhaled.\n\n“That sounds like you.”\n\n“It is unfortunate.”\n\nI looked at him.\n\n“I'm still angry.”\n\n“You should be.”\n\n“I'm not going to pretend the apology fixed it immediately.”\n\n“I did not expect you to.”\n\n“And if you do that again, I will leave again.”\n\nHis face tightened.\n\n“Permanently?”\n\nThere was fear beneath the question.\n\nI let myself see it without exploiting it.\n\n“If it becomes a pattern, yes.”\n\nHe flinched almost invisibly.\n\n“Understood.”\n\nI softened by a fraction.\n\n“Do you want tea?”\n\nHis eyebrows rose.\n\n“Do not read too much into it.”\n\n“I would never.”\n\nI stepped aside.\n\nHe entered.\n\nMy quarters were temporary and impersonal. A bed. A desk. Two chairs. Books everywhere because I had failed to unpack them properly.\n\nSnape looked around.\n\n“You live like this voluntarily?”\n\n“You came here to apologize. Don't ruin it.”\n\nHe sat.\n\nI made tea.\n\nMine with milk. His black.\n\nWhen I handed him the cup, our fingers touched.\n\nHe did not use the moment to make anything easier.\n\nNeither did I.\n\nWe talked about Lucius.\n\nNot the whole history. Enough.\n\nSnape told me they had been friends once, if friendship was the right word for alliances built on ambition and ideology. Lucius had been older. Wealthier. Certain. The kind of person a poor half-blood boy in secondhand robes might mistake for proof that belonging could be earned if he chose the correct side.\n\n“That does not excuse me,” Snape said.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“You do not need to keep saying that.”\n\n“You keep expecting me to.”\n\nHis gaze dropped.\n\n“Perhaps.”\n\nThe tea cooled.\n\nAt some point, our anger became tired instead of sharp.\n\nAt the door, Snape stopped.\n\n“I am sorry,” he said again.\n\nThis time, no explanation followed.\n\nI believed him.\n\n“Thank you.”\n\nHe nodded.\n\nThen he left.\n\nThe next morning, he did not flirt.\n\nNeither did I.\n\nWe worked carefully around the bruise between us.\n\nAt noon, he handed me a jar without setting it down first.\n\nOur fingers touched.\n\nHe waited.\n\nI took it.\n\nThat was not forgiveness all at once.\n\nIt was something better.\n\nThe real test of the apology came the next day.\n\nLucius's second letter arrived at breakfast.\n\nSeverus read it once and went pale with anger. I watched every old defense rise: the tightening jaw, the cold eyes, the urge to turn fear into a weapon before anyone could use it against him.\n\nHe folded the letter.\n\nThen he looked at me.\n\n“I am in a foul mood,” he said.\n\n“Accurate.”\n\n“I am likely to be unpleasant company.”\n\n“Also accurate.”\n\n“I would prefer an hour alone before we work.”\n\nI stared.\n\nHis expression darkened. “Why are you looking at me like that?”\n\n“Because yesterday you sliced me open with three sentences rather than say you were frightened.”\n\n“I was not frightened.”\n\n“You're doing so well. Don't ruin it now.”\n\nHis nostrils flared.\n\nThen, astonishingly, he closed his eyes and corrected himself.\n\n“I was angry,” he said. “And frightened. I remain angry. I would still like an hour.”\n\n“Take two.”\n\nHis eyes opened.\n\n“No punishment?”\n\n“For asking for space?”\n\nA long pause.\n\n“Some people interpret it that way.”\n\n“I don't.”\n\nHe nodded once.\n\nWhen he returned ninety minutes later, he brought tea.\n\nThe apology, I realized, was not the sentence. It was the changed behavior after it.\n\nRepair.","word_count":1709},"after-always-36":{"number":36,"title":"A Better Kind of Classroom","content":"The Board accepted Severus's counterproposal with three amendments, two conditions, and one sentence so pompous that he read it aloud in Minerva's office purely so he could sneer at the punctuation.\n\nMinerva had attached a second sheet beneath the appointment terms. It was less ornate and considerably more important: a procedure for students to report unsafe teaching without first confronting the teacher who had frightened them.\n\nSeverus read it without comment.\n\n“You object?” Minerva asked.\n\n“I object to its implication that the Headmistress intends to devote an afternoon to explaining what a complaint is.”\n\n“The afternoon is already on your schedule.”\n\n“Efficient.”\n\nShe regarded him over her spectacles. “A pupil once had to decide whether it was safer to endure you or report you. I will not build a school in which that is still a reasonable question.”\n\nThe words landed. Severus folded the sheet once, precisely, and set it beside the contract.\n\n“I have not asked you to forget it,” he said.\n\n“No,” Minerva replied. “But you have occasionally behaved as though I should.”\n\nI felt the old instinct to intervene, to make the room kinder for a man I loved. It would have been a mistake. They were two adults discussing a school full of children whose safety mattered more than my discomfort.\n\nSeverus turned back to the document.\n\n“Add a specified response window and an external reviewer when the complaint names the Head of House. Otherwise the process is theatrical.”\n\nMinerva picked up her quill. “Now that is a useful objection.”\n\nHe didn't smile. Later he would, privately, over the ridiculous wording of the Board's other amendments. For now he helped make the rules better and accepted that this was part of being allowed to teach again.\n\n“‘In recognition of exceptional wartime service and with due consideration given to pedagogical concerns arising during prior appointments,’” he quoted, each word sharpened until the phrase sounded like something that ought to be bottled and labeled corrosive. “They have managed to say, in twenty-one words, that I was useful and unpleasant.”\n\nMinerva did not look up from the parchment she was signing. “Both of which are demonstrably true.”\n\nI covered my mouth with one hand.\n\nSeverus turned his head toward me. “Your loyalty is moving.”\n\n“I am loyal to accurate summaries.”\n\n“Then you and the Board have much in common.”\n\nMinerva's quill stopped. The corner of her mouth twitched. “If the pair of you are finished courting over administrative language, we might discuss the actual appointment.”\n\nSeverus went absolutely still.\n\nI made a choking sound.\n\nMinerva looked serenely innocent.\n\nIt was the first time I had ever seen Severus Snape lose an argument before it had properly begun.\n\nHe recovered, of course. He always recovered. His mouth compressed, his eyes narrowed, and he sat straighter in the chair. “Advanced N.E.W.T. seminar. Two afternoons per week. Research access by appointment. No Head of House responsibilities. No disciplinary authority outside my classroom except in immediate matters of safety.”\n\n“Correct.”\n\n“Observed lessons.”\n\n“For every returning member of staff during the first term,” Minerva said. “Not merely you.”\n\nHis eyes flicked toward her.\n\nThat mattered. I could tell by the slight easing of his shoulders.\n\n“Written student complaints routed through the Deputy Headmistress,” she continued. “Again, school-wide. We should have had something of the sort years ago.”\n\nSeverus looked down at the parchment. “Yes.”\n\nNo sarcasm. No defense. Just yes.\n\nI had learned to recognize how much work could live inside one syllable.\n\nThe first class was a week later.\n\nHe pretended not to care that I was there.\n\nI pretended to believe him.\n\nThe dungeon classroom had changed since the war. The broken shelves were repaired. The jars of preserved things that had once made the room feel like the inside of a threat had been reduced to what was genuinely useful for instruction. New lamps threw clean pools of light over every worktable. A charmed ventilation system hummed softly along the stone ceiling.\n\nSeverus inspected it all before the students arrived as if the castle had personally conspired to embarrass him with adequate safety standards.\n\n“You are hovering,” he said without turning.\n\n“I am standing.”\n\n“Near me.”\n\n“That is generally how being in the same room works.”\n\nHis hand paused over a stack of parchment. “You have become intolerable.”\n\n“Terrible influence.”\n\nThat earned me a glance.\n\nIt was not a smile. Severus did not waste perfectly serviceable expressions by giving them names they had not earned. But something warm moved through his eyes before he looked away.\n\nEight students entered at precisely two o'clock.\n\nEvery one of them went quiet when they saw him.\n\nThe silence was not respect. Not entirely. Some of it was fear. Some curiosity. Some the uncomfortable fascination reserved for people whose names had appeared in newspapers too often. One Ravenclaw boy stared openly at the scar crossing Severus's throat until a Hufflepuff girl elbowed him.\n\nSeverus noticed. Of course he noticed.\n\nHe did not punish either of them.\n\n“Sit,” he said.\n\nThey sat.\n\nI took a chair along the back wall beside Professor Sinistra, who was observing for the week. Severus had objected to my presence until I reminded him that I had audited three of his laboratory procedures, survived one experimental vapor exposure, and knew exactly how often he cheated by pretending he did not need rest.\n\nHe had called that argument irrelevant.\n\nThen he had told me where to sit.\n\nThe blackboard filled itself in his narrow handwriting.\n\nADVANCED ANTIDOTE DESIGN: PRINCIPLES OF UNKNOWN TOXINS.\n\nThere were no introductions. No speech about the war. No demand for absolution.\n\nHe placed six unlabeled vials on the demonstration table.\n\n“One contains a harmless saline solution,” he said. “One contains an irritant. Four contain compounds designed to imitate common diagnostic signatures without producing the expected underlying effect. You will not taste them, touch them, sniff them, or attempt whatever inspired act of idiocy is currently forming behind Mr. Bell's eyes.”\n\nThe Ravenclaw boy's hand froze halfway toward his goggles.\n\nA few students laughed.\n\nSeverus's mouth almost moved.\n\n“Your task is not to identify the substances. Your task is to tell me what you would need to know before attempting to identify them.”\n\nA Slytherin girl raised her hand cautiously. “Symptoms?”\n\n“Useful.”\n\n“Time since exposure?” another offered.\n\n“Essential.”\n\n“Known allergies?”\n\n“Better.”\n\nThe answers came faster.\n\nSeverus moved among the tables, robes brushing the floor. He was still intimidating. He was still exacting. When one student gave a lazy answer, he dismantled it in three sentences so precise that I winced in sympathy.\n\nBut he did not humiliate the boy.\n\nThat difference would have been invisible to anyone who did not know him.\n\nI knew him.\n\nHalfway through the exercise, a Gryffindor girl misread a reagent chart and reached for the wrong neutralizer. Severus caught her wrist before she touched it.\n\nShe flinched.\n\nHe released her immediately.\n\n“Miss Patel,” he said, quieter than before, “look again.”\n\nHer face had gone pale.\n\nShe looked.\n\nHer eyes widened. “That would react with the aconite marker.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“I could have—”\n\n“You did not.”\n\nHis voice remained flat, but I saw his fingers flex once at his side. “Why?”\n\nShe swallowed. “Because you stopped me.”\n\n“No.” His gaze moved to the chart. “Because the error was detectable. Safety is not the absence of mistakes. It is the construction of enough barriers that one mistake does not become a catastrophe. Correct your notes.”\n\nShe did.\n\nNo points lost. No cutting remark about incompetence. No public spectacle.\n\nProfessor Sinistra wrote something on her observation form.\n\nSeverus noticed that too.\n\nAfter class, when the students had gone, he stood at his desk pretending to reorganize quills that were already aligned.\n\n“Well?” he asked.\n\n“Do you want my answer or the one you can argue with?”\n\n“The distinction has never prevented you from providing both.”\n\nI came closer.\n\n“You were good.”\n\nHis expression shuttered.\n\n“Don't.”\n\n“I didn't say perfect.”\n\nThe shutters paused.\n\n“You scared Bell half to death over an unlabelled vial.”\n\n“He was about to adjust his goggles.”\n\n“You didn't know that.”\n\n“I knew it in my bones.”\n\nI laughed. “You were still you.”\n\nSomething in him eased.\n\nThat, I realized, was what he had been afraid of.\n\nNot merely failing.\n\nBecoming so careful that there was nothing left of him except apology.\n\nI leaned against the front table. “You can be sharp without using people as whetstones.”\n\nHis eyes lifted to mine.\n\nFor several seconds he said nothing.\n\nThen, very softly, “An appalling metaphor.”\n\n“You understood it.”\n\n“Unfortunately.”\n\nHe gathered the student parchments. One had been left crooked. He straightened it automatically.\n\n“Patel flinched,” he said.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“I saw.”\n\n“I know.”\n\nHis thumb moved along the paper's edge. “There was a time when I would have interpreted that as proof of control.”\n\nI waited.\n\nHe did not need me to rescue him from the sentence.\n\n“At the time,” he continued, “I called fear discipline because it was convenient.”\n\nThe room felt suddenly larger and much quieter.\n\n“You can't change those classes,” I said.\n\n“No.”\n\n“You can change this one.”\n\nHis gaze moved around the dungeon: new lamps, clean air, unthreatening shelves, eight workstations that would fill again next Tuesday.\n\n“Apparently,” he said, “I have agreed to try.”\n\nI reached for his hand.\n\nHe looked down before letting me take it. Consent had become ordinary between us, which made it feel more intimate rather than less.\n\nHis fingers closed around mine.\n\n“You know,” I said, “Minerva was right.”\n\n“That sentence has caused generations of suffering.”\n\n“About us courting over administrative language.”\n\nHe stared at me.\n\nI smiled sweetly.\n\n“Leave my classroom.”\n\n“You're holding my hand.”\n\nHe looked down at our joined fingers as though they had betrayed him.\n\nThen he tightened his grip.\n\n“Detention,” he murmured.\n\n“You don't have disciplinary authority outside immediate safety matters.”\n\nHis eyes narrowed.\n\nI had never enjoyed policy so much in my life.","word_count":1654}}};for(const s of lib.series){const map=changes[s.series_slug]||{};for(const ch of s.chapters){const e=map[ch.id];if(e&&ch.chapter_number===e.number){ch.title=e.title;ch.content=e.content;ch.word_count=e.word_count;}}}})();
